== Subject of the Present Work (1545)

\[Prefixed to the French edition, published at Geneva in 1545.\]

#smallcaps[So] that my readers may be better able to profit by the present work, I wish to point out briefly the benefit they may draw from it. For in doing so I will show them the end at which they ought to aim, and to which they ought to direct their attention in reading it.

Although the Holy Scriptures contain a perfect doctrine, to which nothing can be added—since our Lord has been pleased to unfold in them the infinite treasures of his wisdom—still everyone who is not well acquainted with them needs some guidance and direction as to what he ought to look for in them, so that he may not wander here and there, but keep to a sure path, and so reach the end to which the Holy Spirit invites him.

So it is the duty of those who have received more light from God than others to help the simple in this matter, and, as it were, to lend them a hand, to guide and help them in finding the sum of what God has been pleased to teach us in his word. Now, in writing, this cannot be done better than by treating one after another the principal matters contained in Christian philosophy. For the man who understands these will be ready to make more progress in the school of God in one day than anyone else in three months, since he knows, in great part, to what he should refer each sentence, and has a rule by which to test whatever is put before him.

Seeing, then, how necessary it was to help in this way those who desire to be instructed in the doctrine of salvation, I have tried, according to the ability God has given me, to give myself to this task, and with this aim I have written the present book. I first wrote it in Latin, so that it might serve all who study, of whatever nation they might be; afterwards, wishing to share whatever fruit might be in it with my French countrymen, I translated it into our own language. I dare not speak too strongly in its favor and declare how profitable the reading of it will be, lest I seem to prize my own work too highly. But I may promise this much: it will be a kind of key, opening to all the children of God a right and ready way into the understanding of the sacred volume. So, should our Lord give me from now on the means and opportunity to write some Commentaries, I will use the greatest possible brevity, as there will be no need to make long digressions, since I have, in a way, set out at length all the articles that belong to Christianity.

And since we are bound to acknowledge that all truth and sound doctrine come from God, I will venture to say boldly what I think of this work, acknowledging it to be God’s work rather than mine. To him, indeed, the praise due to it must be given. My opinion of the work, then, is this: I urge all who revere the word of the Lord to read it and diligently fix it in their memory, if they wish, first, to have a summary of Christian doctrine, and second, an introduction to the profitable reading of both the Old and the New Testament. When they have done so, they will know by experience that I have not tried to deceive them with words. If anyone cannot grasp all that it contains, he must not give it up in despair, but keep on reading, hoping that one passage will explain another to him more plainly. Above all, I would urge that, in weighing the proofs I bring from Scripture, the reader go back to Scripture itself.
